/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A discrete-time signal is a sequence x[n], defined only at integer n.
   - The unit impulse is δ[n] = 1 at n = 0 and 0 elsewhere.
   - x[n] = Σk=−∞∞ x[k] δ[n − k]
   - Here x[k] is the value of the input at index k, and δ[n − k] is an impulse placed at n = k.
   - The impulse response h[n] is the output when the input is δ[n] and the system starts at rest.
   - y[n] = Σk=−∞∞ x[k] h[n − k]
   - Here y[n] is the output at index n, x[k] the input at index k, h[n − k] the impulse response flipped and shifted by n, and k the summation index.
   - For finite sequences only the overlapping terms are nonzero.
   - For an infinite h[n] such as aⁿu[n], the same sum is a geometric series; a finite series from 0 to n has the closed form (1 − an+1)/(1 − a).
   - An LTI system is causal exactly when h[n] = 0 for n < 0: the output then uses only present and past inputs.
*/
