/* book: lead: A difference equation is the discrete-time twin of a differential equation. It ties the present output sample to a few past outputs and to present and past inputs, through fixed coefficients. Give it its starting values and a loop computes every later sample, which is what a digital filter does inside a phone or an ECG monitor. The roots of its characteristic equation decide whether the output set */
/* book Figure 5.6.1: Left: the output y[n] passes through two unit delays; the taps y[n − 1] and y[n − 2] are scaled by 0.75 and −0.125 and fed back to the adder. Right: the impulse response this loop produces, 1, 0.75, 0.4375, 0.234, …, which decays because both roots, 0.5 and 0.25, are inside the unit circle. */
