/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A linear constant-coefficient difference equation (LCCDE) of order N is
   - Σk=0N ak y[n − k] = Σm=0M bm x[n − m]
   - Here y[n] is the output, x[n] the input, ak and bm are constants with a0 = 1, N is the order (the largest output delay) and M the largest input delay.
   - Moving the past outputs to the right gives the recursive form y[n] = −a1y[n − 1] − … − aNy[n − N] + b0x[n] + … + bMx[n − M], which computes each sample from numbers already known.
   - If some ak (k ≥ 1) is nonzero, the output feeds back and the system is recursive; its impulse response normally lasts forever, so it is an IIR (infinite impulse response) system.
   - To start the recursion you need N past outputs y[−1], …, y[−N], the initial conditions.
   - Three blocks build every LCCDE: an adder (Σ), a multiplier by a constant, and a unit delay, drawn as z⁻¹ (the symbol comes from Z-transform analysis; here it only means one sample of memory: input y[n], output y[n − 1]).
   - The figure draws worked example 2, y[n] = x[n] + 0.75y[n − 1] − 0.125y[n − 2].
   - Natural response.
   - λN + a1λN−1 + … + aN = 0
*/
